# Fundementals
tips and tricks for OS and cybersec
You are able to change the prompt of kali by "nano ~/.zshrc" | and at the bottom "PROMPT='$(hostname -I | awk "{print \$1}"):%~ %# '" then source "~/.zshrc" every time you change youre ip
if you dont know how to use a tool either put man before it or --help after it example "man ls" "ls --help" some tools you can also use -h
you can also use the apropos command to search for a keyword
uname -a will print the hostname, kernel release and version, machine hardware name, and operating system
find / -type d -name "directory_name" find a directory
find / -type f -name "file_name" 
find / -name "*turo*" will search for all files that have turo in the name. the added * means that there can be letters before and after it, its a wildcard in a way
find / -name "*password*" 2>/dev/null find readable password files
grep -R "password" /home 2>/dev/null find credential files
there are a lot of permission denied, add a 2>/dev/null this hides all errors, DONT ADD A SLASH AT THE END
cd - will take you back one directory
current user echo $USER, home directory echo $HOME, shell echo $SHELL, you can also use the env command to check enviroement variables.
ls -la for permissions and ownerships, ls -li for index numbers so you can find things quickly, or do both. to cite a index number to a file use "find / inum 'index number'"
when creating files or directories you can use the . to specify the path of the directory starts in your current directory
you can use the mv command for both moving and renaming, theres also the cp command.
if theres a command or code that you can run like python or binary the command "which <program>" will tell you the source of the code thats running
for example "which python" is /usr/bin/python etc etc
the command head shows the first 10 lines and tail shows the last 10. you can also use grep to filter large text files. for example ps -a | grep "ssh" will only show files with ssh
to show listening TCP/UDP connections "ss -tulpn"  
grep can be used to filter searching use -v to remove keywords, -c to count the amount of lines, grep "text" filename to search for a keyword, grep "text" * search all files in current diretoctory. 
